
A context read written inside a slot population belongs to the caller, so the
context lowering has to see it there -- the reachability scan, the call-edge
scan and the provider threading each walked a NodeInst's children and skipped
its Slots. The plain slot case is slot_context_read_read.txtar.

An errorBoundary's @error handler is the other body those walks stopped short
of: ir.Rewrite skipped ErrorBoundary.Handler entirely, and
Call.ResolvedHandler only aliases it, so nothing reached a read written there
and the *ir.ContextRead survived lowering to reach codegen, where
irwalk.EvalExpr has no case for one and every platform panicked.

Each target below is the context's value arriving in the handler. fyne and
gtk4 are absent because neither implements errorBoundary at all -- both refuse
the build with `component "errorBoundary" has no <platform> implementation`,
which cmd/sngl/testdata/errorboundary_unimplemented.txt is the assertion on
(issue #112).

The window provides the context, because one nobody provides with a constant
default is folded to that default before the context lowering runs
(passUnprovidedContext), and the handler would never be walked by it.
-- boundary.sngl --
import . "sngl:ui"

output {
    go {
        bubbletea
    }
    kotlin {
        android
    }
    none {
        html
    }
}

context #theme("light")

component main node {
    var caught = ""
    boundary(@error(e) { caught = theme }) {
        button #go(text="Go", @click { error.raise("boom", "") })
    }
    text(value=caught)
}

window {
    theme("light") {
        main
    }
}
